# K4RMA Git Workflow

## Branch safety

- Work exclusively on the current `feat/real-asset-integration` branch.
- Before edits, inspect `git status`, current branch, and relevant diffs.
- Preserve all pre-existing uncommitted and staged changes.
- Never automatically checkout/switch branches, reset, stash, discard, rebase, merge, push, force-push, or rewrite history.
- Never commit unrelated teammate files, temporary caches, generated builds, secrets, or local-only editor settings.

## Required commit behavior

After completing and validating each meaningful, logically coherent feature or bug fix:

1. Inspect the working tree and exact diff.
2. Group all necessary implementation, prefab, scene, asset, metadata, and relevant documentation changes belonging to that single unit of work.
3. Stage ONLY those intended paths or hunks using explicit `git add -- <paths>` or selective staging.
4. Inspect `git diff --cached`.
5. Confirm no unrelated or pre-existing modifications are included.
6. Run the available relevant validation.
7. Create one local commit for the completed logical change.
8. Verify the commit exists and the remaining working tree changes are understood.
9. Continue to the next feature or fix.

Do not commit each file separately. Do not mix unrelated fixes in one commit. Do not defer all completed features into one giant final commit.

If existing user changes overlap the same files, isolate only agent-owned hunks safely. If isolation cannot be guaranteed, do not commit that mixed change; record why and continue safe work.

Do not commit broken, unfinished, or known-failing implementation solely to create progress. A coherent feature may contain multiple scripts, scenes, assets, and `.meta` files.

## Commit messages

Use Conventional Commits:

`<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`

Valid common types:
- `feat` — new gameplay or UI functionality
- `fix` — correction of a defect
- `refactor` — behavior-preserving code restructuring
- `perf` — verified performance improvement
- `test` — automated test changes
- `docs` — documentation-only change
- `chore` — maintenance and project configuration

Typical scopes: `boss`, `combat`, `ui`, `hud`, `assets`, `scene`, `build`.

Rules:
- English only.
- One concise subject line.
- Imperative present tense.
- Lowercase summary.
- No trailing period.
- Describe the actual completed result.
- Prefer a subject under 72 characters.
- No vague messages such as "update", "changes", "fix stuff", or "final version".
- No fabricated claims in commit messages.
- No unnecessary body, footers, or attribution.

Examples:

`feat(boss): implement locked aerial pursuit trajectory`

`fix(boss): clear active hitboxes when attack is interrupted`

`feat(boss): add recoverable exhaustion after final slam`

`feat(ui): redesign Guardian technique selection`

`fix(ui): prevent duplicate confirmation input`

`refactor(combat): separate attack timing from hit detection`

## Prohibited remote actions

NEVER execute:
- `git push`
- `git push --force`
- `git merge`
- `git rebase`
- remote branch modifications
- automated pull request creation

Unless the user explicitly issues a separate authorization, all commits remain local.

## Final reporting

Report:
- Commit hashes and their one-line messages.
- Relevant uncommitted changes, if any.
- Tests and validation completed.
- Remaining blockers.
- Confirmation that nothing was pushed.